WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:06.480
Hi. This is Matt Baker. Today I’m going to&nbsp;
show you a chart from 1858 that displays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:12.000
some statistics about the signers of the&nbsp;
US Declaration of Independence. Every now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:17.520
and then I like to take a look at vintage&nbsp;
pre-computer age charts like this because:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.320
A. I find them to be quite aesthetically&nbsp;
pleasing; and B. They can give us some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:28.400
interesting insight into how people thought&nbsp;
about the past in the past. So, first of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:33.120
here’s what the Declaration of Independence&nbsp;
looks like. As you can see at the bottom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:40.800
it was signed by 56 individuals, with the most&nbsp;
prominent signature be that of John Hancock, who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.640
at the time, was the President&nbsp;
of the Continental Congress.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:50.560
So, what this chart does is it takes those&nbsp;
56 signatures and places them one per row,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:56.320
starting with John Hancock. After Hancock,&nbsp;
the names are arranged by state (shown here),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:02.800
roughly going from north to south. So, we can&nbsp;
see that Pennsylvania had the most signers (9)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:10.640
and Rhode Island had the least (with only 2) but&nbsp;
that all 13 colonies were represented. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:16.720
what the chart does not show is that there were&nbsp;
actually 6 additional British colonies nearby that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:23.200
did not sign. These were, to the north: Quebec,&nbsp;
Nova Scotia, St. John’s Island, and Newfoundland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:28.400
(all of which are now a part of Canada) and&nbsp;
to the south: East Florida and West Florida&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:34.800
(which were first of all, ceded back to Spain but&nbsp;
then eventually did become part of the US later).

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.840
Now, next to the name of each signer, we&nbsp;
also get their date of birth, place of birth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:45.200
education, and profession. I’ll come to back&nbsp;
to the birthdates in a moment but for now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:53.120
take note that, of the 56 signers, only 48 were&nbsp;
born in the colonies. The others included 3 born&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.120 --> 00:02:00.400
in Ireland, 2 in Scotland, 2 in England,&nbsp;
and 1 in Wales. As for their educations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:06.000
31 are marked as having either a collegiate,&nbsp;
academic, or classical education (which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:12.000
I presume, means that they received some sort&nbsp;
of a higher degree). 12 are marked as having a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.600
“liberal” education (which, a note at the bottom&nbsp;
explains as meaning that their education was good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:23.760
but that they didn’t actually graduate from any&nbsp;
particular institution). Finally, 9 are marked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:30.080
as having finished a common schooling only, and&nbsp;
4 are listed as being either home-schooled or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:37.280
self-taught. When it comes to their professions,&nbsp;
the most common, by far, was lawyer, followed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:44.480
merchant. But there were also 4 physicians,&nbsp;
2 clergyman, and a handful of farmers.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:49.600
Now, it is at this point in the chart, that&nbsp;
things gets interesting, at least from a design&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.960
point of view—because we suddenly get a bunch&nbsp;
of lines going either up or down. Basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:01.200
what is happening is that the signers are getting&nbsp;
re-arranged by their birthdates, or age at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:07.200
time of signing, with the oldest now being placed&nbsp;
at the top and the youngest at the bottom. So, for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:13.920
example, although John Hancock is initially listed&nbsp;
at the top, once things are re-arranged by age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:20.480
he ends up way down here at position 39 (meaning&nbsp;
that there were 38 signers who were older than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:26.560
him). This means that, by using the column marked&nbsp;
III, we can quickly determine who the oldest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:32.400
signers were as well as the youngest. So, let’s&nbsp;
try it out. This dot here represents the oldest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.520
person to sign the declaration. If we follow&nbsp;
the line to the left we can find out who that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:43.680
was. If you know your US history, you probably&nbsp;
already guessed that it was Benjamin Franklin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:49.760
(who was aged 70 at the time). What about the&nbsp;
next oldest though? Well, let’s go back up here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:58.000
and follow the line. The 2nd oldest was Stephen&nbsp;
Hopkins, at age 69. He was in poor health, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:04.240
is why his signature appears very shaky. Let’s now&nbsp;
figure out who the youngest person was. Basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:09.840
we just start with this dot here and follow&nbsp;
it left. And we end up with Edward Rutledge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:18.720
who was just 26 years old. The second youngest was&nbsp;
Thomas Lynch Jr., also aged 26. His father, Thomas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:25.760
Lynch Sr., was supposed to sign but became sick,&nbsp;
so his son—Thomas Jr.—had to fill in for him.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:30.800
Let’s now go back up to the top of the chart&nbsp;
and take a look at the next two columns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:36.480
Column IV indicates how many times the signer&nbsp;
was married. So, one dot (like for Benjamin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:43.360
Franklin) means once; whereas two dots (like for&nbsp;
Stephen Hopkins) means twice. And if we look down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:49.600
the list, we can see that no one was married more&nbsp;
than twice. However, there are two rows here with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:55.280
no dots at all, which means that there were two&nbsp;
signers who never married. Let’s figure out who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:03.360
those were. Again, we follow the lines to the left&nbsp;
and we end up with Caesar Rodney and Joseph Hewes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:10.400
In Hewe’s case, he was engaged but his fiancee&nbsp;
died suddenly just a few days before the wedding.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:16.800
Now, being that Column IV represents marriages,&nbsp;
you can probably guess what Column V represents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:21.840
It represents how many children each signer&nbsp;
had. So, the person with the most dots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:27.680
had the most kids. Again, let’s look down the&nbsp;
list and see if anyone stands out. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:34.480
a few with lots of dots but none with more&nbsp;
than this guy here who has 18. Once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:39.680
if we follow the line to the left, we can&nbsp;
find out who it is and in this case it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:46.000
Carter Braxton. He and Benjamin Harrison&nbsp;
(who also signed) were both grandsons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:52.320
of Robert “King” Carter and thus members&nbsp;
of the famous Carter family of Virginia.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:57.600
Okay, moving back up to the top we get another&nbsp;
section of criss-crossed lines. Because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:03.840
now the chart is going to show the order in&nbsp;
which the signers died. So, this dot here (in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:08.960
Column VI) represents the first person to&nbsp;
pass away. Following the line to the left,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:13.760
we first learn that this person had eight&nbsp;
children and only married once. And if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:19.920
keep going we find out that the person was&nbsp;
John Morton. In fact he died less than 9&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:25.920
months after the signing of the Declaration&nbsp;
of Independence, probably from tuberculosis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:31.920
Let’s now go back up and trace the second&nbsp;
line to find out who the next person was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:40.640
to pass away. That person was… doot do do do do…&nbsp;
Button Gwinnett, who died just one month later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:46.480
Gwinnett is notable for two reasons. First of&nbsp;
all, his name appears on the declaration first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:53.280
if we start at the top left. Second, he’s&nbsp;
the only one of the signers to die by duel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:59.520
While the Burr–Hamilton duel is more well&nbsp;
know, Hamilton was not one of the signers.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:03.920
Now, what about the last person to die?&nbsp;
Well, let’s go to the bottom and follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:11.120
this line here. It leads to Charles Carroll&nbsp;
of Carrollton, who died in 1832 and who was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:16.960
the only Catholic to sign the declaration. His&nbsp;
signature is interesting because it’s the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:22.160
one that includes a place of residence as&nbsp;
a part of the name. He did this in order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:28.560
to distinguish himself from his father, who was&nbsp;
known as Charles Carroll of Annapolis. Anyhow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:34.320
Charles Carroll of Carrollton is also notable for&nbsp;
one other reason but I’m going to skip that for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:41.360
now in order to point out the 2nd and 3rd last&nbsp;
persons to die because they happen to be the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:48.720
most important individuals on the entire chart&nbsp;
and curiously, they died on the exact same day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:56.640
which happened to be July the 4th, exactly 50&nbsp;
years after the signing of the declaration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:03.840
If we zoom out and trace those two lines,&nbsp;
we get… John Adams and Thomas Jefferson:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:10.240
the only two signers who ended up serving as&nbsp;
Presidents (the 2nd and 3rd, respectively).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:15.280
Take note that George Washington (the 1st&nbsp;
President) was not one of the signers, nor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:21.840
was James Madison (the 4th President), although&nbsp;
they both later signed the U.S. constitution.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:27.600
Okay, let me now tell you about the last four&nbsp;
columns. Columns VII and VIII indicate whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:33.520
or not the signer left behind a wife or any&nbsp;
children. And the final two columns indicate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:39.440
age to which the signer lived. And this is where&nbsp;
we circle back to Charles Carroll of Carrollton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:45.200
because, not only was he the last signer to&nbsp;
die, he was also the one who lived the longest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:51.920
making it to age 95. As for the one who lived&nbsp;
the shortest life, well, let’s take a look.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:58.480
We have to start at this dot here and then&nbsp;
trace the line all the way up here (because he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:05.920
was the 4th to die) and then way back down here,&nbsp;
because he was the second youngest to sign. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:12.000
oh, we’ve already talked about him. That was&nbsp;
Thomas Lynch Jr. (the one who signed because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:17.200
his father got sick). Well, just four years&nbsp;
later, he set sail for the West Indies but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:24.480
the ship he set sail on… disappeared. So he was&nbsp;
declared, at age 30, to have been “lost at sea”.

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:29.600
Now, if you like charts and you’re interested in&nbsp;
